A 12V DC air pump with higher flow rate was purchased (https://www.walmart.com/ip/OT-DC-AIR-PUMP/7266117287?wl13=3255&wmlspartner=wlpa&selectedSellerId=0)

This air pump has a flow rate of 680L/minute, which is far greater than the 10-12 L/minute that we are getting from the previous 12V pump 
(https://skycraftsurplus.com/products/micro-air-pump-12-volt-dc.html) and the 2.5L/minute that we were getting from the pump before that, which was a 4.5V
(and the 2.5Lminute that we were getgging). However, this new pump has a much lower static pressure of 0.5 PSI, whereas the previous 12V pump had a static pressure of 29 PSI and the 
4.5V pump has a static pressure of 7.98 PSI. We purchased this pump because we were having issues with flow rate, so we wanted a pump with more flow rate. 

We tested the performance of this motor, but discovered that the static pressure was incredibly important to the output air flow rate. There was almost no measurable airflow at the 
harmonica end. This is becuase pressure differential is the imortant factor for driving air through a solenoid, not air flow rate. From this test, we can conclude that the limiting 
factor of our design is likely the solenoids and not the air pumps. This is because our air pumps already have such a high static pressure that we are not likely to have exceeded
it in our design. A solenoid requiring a lower pressure differential would be the solution to this problem, but such solenoids are prohibitively expensive ($8-12 each, as compared
to the $4 solenoids we are currently using). A solution could be to 3D print a different solenoid head piece, which could have a larger opening for air and therefore give more 
airflow. 
